the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a block* is cluttered.
    *a block* is a clear.
    *a block* is supported.
    *a block* is table.
    *a thing* stack *a second thing* with *a third thing*.
    *a thing* is the above of *a second thing*.
    *a block* is a block.
    *a thing* on *a second thing*.

the knowledge base blocks includes:

the text of the ruleset is at "sources/blocks.rules".

a block is cluttered if
    a thing on the block.

a block is a clear if
    the block is a block
    and it is not the case that the block is cluttered.

a block is supported if
    the block on a thing.

a block is table if
    the block is a block
    and it is not the case that the block is supported.

a thing stack a second thing with a third thing if
    the thing on the second thing
    and the second thing on the third thing.

a thing is the above of a second thing if
    the thing on the second thing.

a thing is the above of a second thing if
    the thing on a third thing
    and the third thing is the above of the second thing.

scenario dataset is, as stated in the dataset:
    a is a block.
    b is a block.
    c is a block.
    d is a block.
    e is a block.
    a on b.
    b on c.
    d on e.
    above expects answers ["a is the above of b", "a is the above of c", "b is the above of c", "d is the above of e"].
    clear expects answers ["a is a clear", "d is a clear"].
    cluttered expects answers ["b is cluttered", "c is cluttered", "e is cluttered"].
    stack expects answers ["a stack b with c"].
    supported expects answers ["a is supported", "b is supported", "d is supported"].
    table expects answers ["c is table", "e is table"].

query above is:
    which thing is the above of which second thing.

query clear is:
    which block is a clear.

query cluttered is:
    which block is cluttered.

query stack is:
    which thing stack which second thing with which third thing.

query supported is:
    which block is supported.

query table is:
    which block is table.

